 "transcript": {
  "answer": "Women's job market in Victoria has loosened over the past six months, though employment is still up on two years ago. Figures are ABS seasonally adjusted monthly data, latest September 2026.\n\n- **Unemployment rate: 5.1%** (September 2026). It was 4.5% in July 2024 and bottomed around 3.8–3.9% in late 2024 and spring 2025. It has risen since March 2026, peaking at 5.2% in July 2026.\n- **Employed: about 1.84 million women**, up roughly 77,000 from July 2024. Numbers have been roughly flat since late 2025.\n- **Participation rate: 63.6%**, up about 0.5 percentage points over two years, though it dipped in early 2026 and has since recovered.\n- **Employment-to-population ratio: 60.4%**, close to where it was two years ago. It fell in early 2026 and has only partly recovered.\n\nFor comparison, men in Victoria in September 2026 had an unemployment rate of 5.0%, a participation rate of 71.5% and an employment-to-population ratio of 67.9%. Unemployment rates are similar for women and men, but women's participation is about 8 percentage points lower, so a much smaller share of women are in work.\n\nMonthly figures bounce around, so the direction over several months matters more than any single reading. I can pull full-time versus part-time splits or a longer history if you want them.",
